WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 288: King Arthur: The Once and Future King, Camelot, and the Celtic Legend of Excalibur

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.360
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.480
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:13.760
[Rebecca] Today we begin our grand new series, The Kings and Queens of England, tracing the astonishing monarchs who shaped the British realm.

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:21.840
[Rebecca] Before we explore the historical record of crown sovereigns, we examine the immortal legend that hovers like mist over the British imagination.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:29.360
[Rebecca] History reminds us that while Arthur is a figure of folklore rather than verified fact, no king has cast a longer shadow.

6
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the legend of King Arthur.

7
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:51.280
[George] When the Roman legions withdrew from the misty shores of Britannia in 410 AD, the province collapsed into a fractured patchwork of Celtic chiefdoms beset by violent raids from Picts, Scots, and encroaching Germanic warbands of Angles, Saxons, and Jutes.

8
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.480
[George] In this dark crucible of civilizational collapse, memory morphed into myth.

9
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:05.920
[George] Modern historians and archaeologists emphasize that there is no verified contemporary historical evidence for a single crowned king named Arthur ruling Britain.

10
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:18.640
[George] Instead, the legend likely coalesced around a shadowy, amalgamated Celtic battle leader, a Ducks Bellorum, who rallied Romano-British cavalry against the pagan invaders in the 5th or 6th century.

11
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:40.080
[George] The Welsh monk Nennius in his 9th century Historia Britannum first recorded the heroic tradition, listing twelve legendary battles fought by Arthur, culminating in the Titanic Clash at Mount Badon, where Arthur was fabled to have carried the image of the Virgin Mary upon his shield and single handedly slain 960 enemy warriors.

12
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.680
[George] The historical consensus of modern archaeology and textual criticism is unequivocal.

13
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.920
[George] Arthur was not an anointed, crown-wearing sovereign seated upon a golden throne in a stone castle.

14
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.320
[George] Late antique Britain in the 5th century possessed no unified crown.

15
00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:06.040
[George] Roman paved roads were cracking, villa hypocosts had collapsed into weed-choked rubble, and urban Roman culture had evaporated into subsistence pastoralism.

16
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:21.240
[George] The 6th century British monk Gildas, writing his sorrowful sermon Du Exidio et Conquistu Britanniae barely a generation after the events, mentions the decisive British victory at Mount Badon, but never once mentions the name Arthur.

17
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:32.840
[George] This silence confirms that Arthur's transformation from a local Romano-Celtic war captain into the supreme Christian king of European chivalry, was a masterpiece of medieval literary mythmaking.

18
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:47.000
[George] In the 12th century, the Oxford cleric Geoffrey of Monmouth published his sensational Historia Regum Britanniae, weaving fragmentary Welsh bardic tales into a grand pseudo-historical epic that enchanted medieval Europe.

19
00:02:47.000 --> 00:03:00.200
[George] Geoffrey introduced the world to Arthur's mythic conception, born at the rugged sea-swept fortress of Tintagel in Cornwall through the sorcery of the wizard Merlin, who disguised King Other Pendragon as the Duke of Cornwall.

20
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:11.560
[George] According to the legend popularized by French romances and Sir Thomas Mallory, when other died without an acknowledged heir, a miraculous anvil holding a gleaming broadsword appeared outside St.

21
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.080
[George] Paul's Cathedral in London, bearing gold letters.

22
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:21.000
[George] Whoso pulleth out this sword of this stone and anvil, is rightwise king born of all England.

23
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:30.120
[George] While proud barons and mighty knights failed to budge the blade, young squire Arthur withdrew it effortlessly, proving that divine right and inner purity.

24
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.600
[George] Rather than brute martial force, ordained his sacred sovereignty.

25
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:51.760
[George] The character of Merlin, conceived in Welsh folklore as the wild prophet Mia Dinwilt, who ran mad in the Caledonian forest after a bloody battle, was refashioned by medieval French romancers into a sage architect of divine destiny who guided the young monarch through moral trials.

26
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.520
[George] The anvil and the stone were potent Christian metaphors.

27
00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:06.400
[George] The anvil represented the forge of earthly adversity, while the unyielding stone symbolized the unshakable foundation of the church upon which the sovereign's moral authority must rest.

28
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:19.360
[George] By withdrawing the sacred blade without a drop of blood shed, young Arthur proved that legitimate kingship does not flow from aristocratic lineage or violent usurpation, but from moral purity and divine election.

29
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:29.280
[George] When Arthur broke his first sword during combat with King Pelinor, Merlin led the young monarch deep into an enchanted forest to the shores of a tranquil, misty lake.

30
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:38.320
[George] From the glassy surface rose a mystical arm clothed in white samite, holding aloft the legendary sword Excalibur, the Celtic Caliburn.

31
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:46.400
[George] The Lady of the Lake bestowed the blade upon Arthur, warning him that its woven scabbard was far more precious than the razor-sharp steel.

32
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.600
[George] As long as he wore the magical sheath, he would lose no blood from any wound in battle.

33
00:04:51.600 --> 00:05:08.800
[George] Forged in the mystic otherworld of Avalon, Excalibur became the quintessential symbol of righteous authority, gleaming like thirty torches on the battlefield as Arthur united the warring factions of Britain, established peace, and drove the Saxon invaders back to the eastern marshes.

34
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:24.240
[George] The recurring motif of the mystical lake connects the Arthurian legend to ancient Celtic pre-Christian religious rituals, where bronze swords, shields and gold cauldrons were ritually broken and cast into sacred lakes and rivers as votive offerings to water deities.

35
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:37.000
[George] The Lady of the Lake, known in French romances as Nimu or Vivienne, was an otherworldly guardian of divine balance, bestowing the magical blade only on the condition that Arthur govern with righteous restraint.

36
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.880
[George] The magical scabbard served as a continuous spiritual warning to the king.

37
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:50.840
[George] Military violence was an unfortunate necessity of fallen earthly governance, but true sovereign greatness lay in preserving life rather than taking it.

38
00:05:50.840 --> 00:06:04.440
[George] To banish jealousy among his proud warrior chieftains, Arthur's father-in-law, King Leodigrants, gifted the young monarch a massive circular table of polished oak as a wedding present upon his marriage to the noble Guinevere.

39
00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:10.280
[George] The round table was an extraordinary philosophical innovation in the folklore of medieval kingship.

40
00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:17.480
[George] Possessing no head and no foot, every knight seated around its circumference was granted equal dignity and fellowship.

41
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:30.200
[George] Gathered at Arthur's shining capital of Camelot, the knights of the round table, including Sir Lancelot of the Lake, Sir Gawain, Sir Galahad, and Sir Percival, swore the sacred Pentecostal oath.

42
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:46.520
[George] They pledged never to commit murder or treason, to flee cruelty, to aid widows and damsels in distress, and to use their martial strength exclusively in defense of justice, creating the romantic template of Christian chivalry that inspired European literature for centuries.

43
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:53.560
[George] The architectural geometry of the round table was a radical philosophical subversion of European feudal hierarchy.

44
00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:59.880
[George] In medieval banquet halls, the king sat elevated beneath a velvet canopy on a high dais.

45
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.920
[George] With lesser nobles placed below the salt according to strict hereditary rank.

46
00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:17.360
[George] Arthur's circular table, fabled to accommodate one hundred and forty knights, eliminated all physical markers of precedence, demanding that the sovereign look his comrades in the eye as an equal brother in arms.

47
00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:34.880
[George] The chivalric code formulated at Camelot was a noble attempt by medieval Christian moralists to tame the brutal, rapacious violence of feudal warlords, sublimating raw martial aggression into an ethical code of courtly honor, protection for the weak, and self-sacrificing service.

48
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:52.640
[George] The Golden Age of Camelot was crowned and spiritually destabilized by the ultimate medieval Christian quest, the search for the sangrial or holy grail, the sacred chalice used by Jesus Christ at the Last Supper and brought to Glastonbury in Britain by Joseph of Arimathea.

49
00:07:52.640 --> 00:08:08.560
[George] As a blinding celestial radiance illuminated the great hall of Camelot, and the mystical vessel drifted through the air covered in white samite, the knights rose as one, vowing to disperse into the uncharted wilderness until they beheld the sacred mystery face to face.

50
00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.840
[George] The quest for the grail tested the souls of the knights.

51
00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:21.840
[George] Worldly prowess and battlefield slaughter were rendered worthless, with only the pure-hearted Sir Galahad achieving the beatific vision, ascending directly into heaven.

52
00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:28.560
[George] The quest depleted the fellowship of Camelot, leaving the court exhausted and vulnerable to worldly sins.

53
00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:37.360
[George] The Grail Quest introduced a tragic, irreconcilable tension between earthly chivalric glory and the demands of Christian spiritual transcendence.

54
00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:47.520
[George] While knights like Lancelot had spent lifetimes perfecting swordplay and courtly love, the Grail demanded total ascetic self abnegation and absolute chastity.

55
00:08:47.520 --> 00:09:01.880
[George] When Lancelot approached the door of the grail chamber in the castle of Carbonac, he was struck down by a searing divine wind, blinded and paralyzed for twenty-four days, one day for each year he had lived in sinful adulterous love with Guinevere.

56
00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:15.880
[George] The departure of Sir Galahad, who achieved the grail vision and died in ecstasy in the holy city of Saras, left Camelot spiritually bereft, an aging court clinging to worldly vanities as the shadows of betrayal lengthened.

57
00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.400
[George] The tragic downfall of Camelot was ignited from within.

58
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:28.680
[George] The illicit, passionate love between Arthur's beloved Queen Guinevere and his greatest champion, Sir Lancelot, shattered the moral harmony of the realm.

59
00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:42.040
[George] Exploiting the scandal, Arthur's treacherous, illegitimate son and nephew, Mordred, seized control of London while Arthur was campaigning abroad, proclaiming himself king and attempting to force Guinevere into marriage.

60
00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:50.280
[George] Returning to reclaim his kingdom, Arthur met Mordred's rebel host upon the desolate, fog-shrouded field of Camlan in Cornwall.

61
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:57.720
[George] The battle was a mutual apocalyptic slaughter, an entire generation of noble knights perished in a single bloody day.

62
00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:10.760
[George] In the climactic duel, Arthur impaled Mordred with his spear, but as Mordred died, he struck Arthur's skull a mortal blow with his sword, shattering the king's helm and ending the golden brotherhood of Camelot.

63
00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:16.440
[George] The tragic romance between Lancelot and Guinevere was not merely an illicit bedroom scandal.

64
00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:24.840
[George] It was the systemic unraveling of the moral contract that bound the round table together, pitting brother knights against one another in blood feuds.

65
00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:41.480
[George] Sir Gawain, maddened by grief when Lancelot accidentally slew his unarmed brothers Gareth and Gaharis, during Guinevere's rescue from the stake, demanded relentless vengeance, forcing Arthur to besiege Lancelot's castle in France while Mordred seized the English throne.

66
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:57.840
[George] The Battle of Camlen was described by Celtic bards as a mournful slaughter so terrible that only three warriors survived, the clash of swords and shattered shields echoing over the Cornwall moors like the dying gasp of a golden age that humanity was too corrupt to preserve.

67
00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:10.240
[George] As Arthur lay bleeding to death on the shores of Camlan, attended only by the weeping Sir Bediver, the dying king ordered his last remaining knight to carry Excalibur to the water's edge and cast it into the lake.

68
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:19.520
[George] Twice Bediver hesitated, seduced by the precious jewels in the golden hilt, but on his third attempt, he flung the blade far into the mist.

69
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:30.240
[George] Before it struck the surface, an arm clothed in white samite rose from the depths, caught the sword by the hilt, flourished it three times, and vanished beneath the waves.

70
00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:43.040
[George] A morning barge draped in black silk glided to the shore, steered by weeping queens, including Morgan Le Fay, who bore the wounded king away to the mystical islandvale of Avalon to be healed of his grievous wounds.

71
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:50.240
[George] Inscribed upon his fabled tomb, as Sir Thomas Mallory wrote, stands the eternal promise of British folklore.

72
00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:57.520
[George] Hick Jasset Arthuris, Rex Quandum, Rix Futurus, here lies Arthur, the once and future king.

73
00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:10.960
[George] Sir Bediver's threefold hesitation to cast Excalibur into the deep waters mirrored the disciples' sleep in Gethsamain, illustrating the frail human temptation to cling to earthly relics of power rather than obey divine commands.

74
00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:25.040
[George] The three crowned queens who bore Arthur away into the sunset mists of Avalon represented the enduring Celtic goddesses of sovereignty, healing, and prophecy, transporting the mortally wounded king beyond the reach of human mortality.

75
00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:43.240
[George] For centuries, Welsh bards and English poets clung to the prophecy that Arthur did not truly die, but sleeps in an enchanted cavern beneath the hills, waiting to awaken and draw Excalibur once more in Britain's hour of direced peril, an eternal symbol of hope in the triumph of justice.

76
00:12:53.560 --> 00:13:02.440
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the true historical sovereign who saved Anglo Saxon England from the Viking Fury, King Alfred the Great.

77
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:05.400
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

